# The on-screen keyboard: what each button does, and the four panels the layer key walks.
#
# The pad is the point here. B backs out of this screen the way it backs out of every other one
# and X is the backspace, which is where a controller keyboard puts them; the two triggers the
# case carries are the shift. The layer key at the bottom left steps the same ring the shoulders
# do - abc, ABC, symbols, then a page of emoji at a time - which is how a `/` and a face are
# both two presses from the letters rather than a hunt.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 140

tab messages
hold 400

# Into a conversation, then straight to the keyboard with Y.
key down 4
key a
hold 600
key y
hold 700

# Type "ok" on the lower layer.
key down
key right 8
key a
hold 300
key down
key left 7
key a
hold 700

# X is the backspace: the "k" goes.
key x
hold 800

# A trigger shifts for one character, exactly as it would on a phone.
key r2
hold 500
key a
hold 800

# The shoulders step the panel ring. Once for the symbols layer, where the sentence punctuation
# row now carries "/" beside the comma and the full stop - an MQTT topic and a URL are the two
# things typed here that need it.
key x
key r1
key r1
hold 900
key down 2
key right 7
key a
hold 900

# Once more for the emoji, which the client could draw on a node's name long before it could
# type one. Three pages of forty, stepped by the same two buttons.
key r1
hold 900
key a
hold 600
key r1
hold 900
key r1
hold 900

# B leaves, and what was typed comes back with it - the compose sheet's draft row is holding it.
key b
hold 1400
